"Surah al-Infitar was revealed in Mecca and consists of nineteen verses, eighty words, and three hundred and twenty-seven letters. The surah addresses the terror of the Day of Resurrection and human responsibility.\n\nIn the first four verses, the portents of the Day of Resurrection are enumerated: when the sky is cleft asunder, when the stars are scattered, when the seas are burst forth, and when the graves are overturned. These events depict the moment the cosmic order is disrupted. In the fifth verse, it is stated that everyone will know what they have sent forth and what they have left behind in this world. This means that a person will see the recompense for the good or evil they have done.\n\nIn the sixth verse, addressing man, it is said: 'O man! What has deceived you concerning your Generous Lord?' This question challenges man's falling into rebellion by relying on Allah's grace. In the seventh and eighth verses, this generosity is recalled by recounting Allah's creating man, giving him a proportionate form, and fashioning him in whatever shape He willed. Man ought to be grateful for these blessings of creation and must not succumb to pride.\n\nIn the ninth verse, by the statement, 'Nay, but you deny the Judgment,' people's denial of the reckoning in the Hereafter is criticized. In verses ten through twelve, it is stated that there are guardian angels over every person who record everything that is done. These angels are described as 'kiraman katibin,' meaning noble scribes.\n\nIn the thirteenth and fourteenth verses, it is stated that the righteous will be in bliss and the wicked will be in Hell. Verses fifteen and sixteen emphasize that the wicked will enter Hell on the Day of Judgment and will not be able to escape from it. This shows that the recompense for the good and evil deeds done will certainly be rendered.\n\nIn the last three verses, the gravity of the Day of Judgment is emphasized through questioning: 'And what will make you realize what the Day of Judgment is?' In the nineteenth verse, the characteristic of that day is explained: no soul will have the power to help another, and the command will belong entirely to Allah. This recalls the dread of the Day of the Hereafter and the absolute sovereignty of Allah."